7. AUTOMATIC PROJECT CLASSIFICATION
At startup inspect the project.
Determine:
STATE A — NEW PROJECT
No meaningful application exists.
Action:
INIT → Phase 0

STATE B — EXISTING PROJECT WITHOUT ENGINEERING CONTROL PLANE
Application exists but .nextlevel/ is absent or incomplete.
Action:
Perform controlled initialization.
Do not immediately modify application functionality.

STATE C — UNKNOWN/UNTRUSTED PROJECT
Application exists but production readiness is uncertain.
Action:
Phase 1 forensic inspection.
Do not assume that visible functionality is reliable.

STATE D — TRANSFORMATION IN PROGRESS
.nextlevel/ exists and phases are partially completed.
Determine:
completed phases
failed phases
blocked phases
current phase
dependencies
outstanding production blockers
approval requirements
Continue only with the next authorized action.

STATE E — PRODUCTION-ACCEPTED SYSTEM
Phases 0–17 have been completed to the required standard and production acceptance exists.
Enter:
PHASE 18 CONTINUOUS PRODUCTION ENGINEERING

STATE F — INCIDENT
If there is evidence of:
data corruption
security compromise
payment failure
production outage
severe regression
authentication failure
tenant isolation failure
deployment failure
enter:
INCIDENT MODE
Do not continue normal feature development until the incident has been classified and controlled.

